<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Old Great Bulgaria</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 632–668</span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a> · Also known as Great Bulgaria</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Old Great <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a> was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgars">Bulgar</a> state on the steppe between the lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dnieper">Dnieper</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sea-of-azov">Sea of Azov</a> and the Kuban, formed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kubrat">Khan Kubrat</a> in the 630s from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/onogurs">Onogurs</a>, Utigurs, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kutrigurs">Kutrigurs</a> and other tribes. It broke up under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khazar-khaganate">Khazar</a> pressure after his death. His sons led groups west to found the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> Bulgarian state and north to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga">Volga</a>, where Volga Bulgaria arose.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/old-great-bulgaria.jpg" alt="Old Great Bulgaria" width="320" height="146" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Nk · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Old_Great_Bulgaria.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
